 {"name": "RingTheory.CohenMacaulay.notMem_minimalPrimes_of_regularLocal_succ", "kind": "lemma", "signature": "private lemma notMem_minimalPrimes_of_regularLocal_succ (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R] {k : \u2115} (hdim : (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R).spanFinrank = k + 1) (x : R) (hxMem : x \u2208 IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) (hxNotSq : x \u2209 (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) ^ 2) (hIH : \u2200 (R' : Type u) [CommRing R'] [IsLocalRing R'] [IsNoetherianRing R'] [IsRegularLocalRing R'], (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R').spanFinrank = k \u2192 IsDomain R') : Ideal.span ({x} : Set R) \u2209 minimalPrimes R", "doc": "**`(x)` is not a minimal prime in the regular-local inductive step.** For a regular local Noetherian ring `R` of `spanFinrank \ud835\udd2a = k + 1` and `x \u2208 \ud835\udd2a \\ \ud835\udd2a\u00b2`, the ideal `Ideal.span {x}` is *not* a minimal prime of `R`.  The strong-induction hypothesis `hIH` is taken as an explicit argument (universally quantified over the ring `R'` at dimension `k`), so the lemma can be invoked inside `isDomain_of_regularLocal`'s `succ` arm without requiring `IsDomain R` (which is the goal being proved there).", "library": "Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild", "file": "MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild/AlgebraicJacobian/Algebra/ABRegularDomain.lean", "line": 94}
-{"name": "RingTheory.CohenMacaulay.isDomain_of_regularLocal", "kind": "lemma", "signature": "lemma isDomain_of_regularLocal (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R] : IsDomain R", "doc": "**Regular local Noetherian rings are domains** (Stacks tag 00NP). Every regular local Noetherian ring is an integral domain.  This is the consumer-facing implication needed to close `exists_isRegular_of_regularLocal` (and through it `CohenMacaulay.of_regular`).  The body is a strong induction on `spanFinrank \ud835\udd2a R`:  * Base case `n = 0` \u2192 `isDomain_of_isLocalRing_of_spanFinrank_maximalIdeal_eq_zero` (\ud835\udd2a collapses to `\u22a5`, `R` is a field, hence a domain). * Inductive step `n = k + 1`: pick `x \u2208 \ud835\udd2a \\ \ud835\udd2a\u00b2` via `exists_notMemSq_of_spanFinrank_pos`; then `R/(x)` is regular local of `spanFinrank = k` (`regularLocal_quotient_isRegularLocal_of_notMemSq`), hence a domain by the IH, hence `(x)` is prime. Take a minimal prime `\ud835\udd2d \u2286 (x)`: the `x \u2209 \ud835\udd2d` branch closes via `\ud835\udd2d \u2286 x\u00b7\ud835\udd2d` + Nakayama (`\ud835\udd2d = \u22a5`, so `\u22a5` is prime); the `x \u2208 \ud835\udd2d` branch is refuted by `notMem_minimalPrimes_of_regularLocal_succ`.", "library": "Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild", "file": "MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild/AlgebraicJacobian/Algebra/ABRegularDomain.lean", "line": 278}
+{"name": "RingTheory.CohenMacaulay.isDomain_of_regularLocal", "kind": "lemma", "signature": "lemma isDomain_of_regularLocal (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R] : IsDomain R", "doc": "**Regular local Noetherian rings are domains** (Stacks tag 00NP). Every regular local Noetherian ring is an integral domain.  This is the consumer-facing implication needed to close `exists_isRegular_of_regularLocal` (and through it `CohenMacaulay.of_regular`).  The body is a strong induction on `spanFinrank \ud835\udd2a R`:  * Base case `n = 0` \u2192 `isDomain_of_isLocalRing_of_spanFinrank_maximalIdeal_eq_zero` (\ud835\udd2a collapses to `\u22a5`, `R` is a field, hence a domain). * Inductive step `n = k + 1`: pick `x \u2208 \ud835\udd2a \\ \ud835\udd2a\u00b2` via `exists_notMemSq_of_spanFinrank_pos`; then `R/(x)` is regular local of `spanFinrank = k` (`regularLocal_quotient_isRegularLocal_of_notMemSq`), hence a domain by the IH, hence `(x)` is prime. Take a minimal prime `\ud835\udd2d \u2286 (x)`: the `x \u2209 \ud835\udd2d` branch closes via `\ud835\udd2d \u2286 x\u00b7\ud835\udd2d` + Nakayama (`\ud835\udd2d = \u22a5`, so `\u22a5` is prime); the `x \u2208 \ud835\udd2d` branch is refuted by `notMem_minimalPrimes_of_regularLocal_succ`.       * Provenance: REFERENCE.", "library": "Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild", "file": "MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild/AlgebraicJacobian/Algebra/ABRegularDomain.lean", "line": 286}
 {"name": "AlgHom.ext_of_isLocalization_pi", "kind": "theorem", "signature": "theorem AlgHom.ext_of_isLocalization_pi (M : \u03b9 \u2192 Submonoid A) [\u2200 i, IsLocalization (M i) (S i)] {\u03c6 \u03c8 : (\u2200 i, S i) \u2192\u2090[A] C} (h : \u2200 i, \u03c6 (Pi.single i 1) = \u03c8 (Pi.single i 1)) : \u03c6 = \u03c8", "doc": "**The pi-ext lemma.**  Two `A`-algebra maps out of a finite product of localizations of `A` that agree on the coordinate idempotents `Pi.single i 1` are equal.  Proof sketch: fix `i` and let `\u03b5 := \u03c6 (Pi.single i 1)`, an idempotent with the same value for `\u03c8`.  The map `y \u21a6 \u03c6 (Pi.single i y)`, corestricted to the quotient `C \u29f8 Ideal.span {1 - \u03b5}`, is a *unital* `A`-algebra map out of the localization `S i` (unitality is `\u03c6 (single i 1) = \u03b5 \u21a6 1`), hence agrees with the one for `\u03c8` by `IsLocalization.ringHom_ext`.  Therefore `\u03c6 (single i y) - \u03c8 (single i y) \u2208 span {1 - \u03b5}`; multiplying by `\u03b5` and using `\u03c6 (single i y) * \u03b5 = \u03c6 (single i y)` (from `single i y * single i 1 = single i y`) gives `\u03c6 (single i y) = \u03c8 (single i y)`.  Conclude by `x = \u2211 i, Pi.single i (x i)` (`Finset.univ_sum_single`).", "library": "Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild", "file": "MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild/AlgebraicJacobian/Algebra/PiLocalization.lean", "line": 62}
 {"name": "Module.Flat.pi_of_finite", "kind": "theorem", "signature": "theorem Module.Flat.pi_of_finite [\u2200 i, Module.Flat A (S i)] : Module.Flat A (\u2200 i, S i)", "doc": "A finite product of flat modules is flat.", "library": "Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild", "file": "MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild/AlgebraicJacobian/Algebra/PiLocalization.lean", "line": 132}
 {"name": "Module.FaithfullyFlat.pi_of_span_eq_top", "kind": "theorem", "signature": "theorem Module.FaithfullyFlat.pi_of_span_eq_top (f : \u03b9 \u2192 A) [\u2200 i, IsLocalization.Away (f i) (S i)] (hspan : Ideal.span (Set.range f) = \u22a4) : Module.FaithfullyFlat A (\u2200 i, S i)", "doc": "**A finite product of localizations along a covering family is faithfully flat**: if `Ideal.span (Set.range f) = \u22a4`, then `\u2200 i, A[1/f i]` is a faithfully flat `A`-algebra.  Proof sketch: flatness is `IsLocalization.flat` plus `Module.Flat.pi_of_finite`.  For faithfulness use `Module.FaithfullyFlat.iff_flat_and_proper_ideal`: a proper ideal `I` lies in a maximal `m`, and since `span (range f) = \u22a4` some `f i \u2209 m`.  If `I \u2022 (\u22a4 : Submodule A (\u2200 i, S i)) = \u22a4` then, applying the (surjective) coordinate projection and `Submodule.map_smul''`, also `I \u2022 (\u22a4 : Submodule A (S i)) = \u22a4`, hence `m \u2022 \u22a4 = \u22a4`.  Map along the ring map `\u03c7 : S i \u2192+* A \u29f8 m` obtained from `IsLocalization.Away.lift` (the class of `f i` in the field `A \u29f8 m` is nonzero, hence invertible): the equation `1 = \u2211 a\u2096 \u2022 s\u2096` with `a\u2096 \u2208 m` maps to `1 = 0`, a contradiction.", "library": "Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild", "file": "MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild/AlgebraicJacobian/Algebra/PiLocalization.lean", "line": 150}
